{
  "requirement": "A named item's `name_span` is `Some` with the span to highlight for its name\nin the original source snapshot. The highlighted text may differ from the\nname in the selector. Use `selector` to address the item. The file root and\nanonymous items have `name_span` set to `None`. `OutlineItem::new` starts with\n`None`. Language providers use `with_name_span` to set the highlight within\nthe item’s own span.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover each obligation in `requirements.sdk.outlines.name_span`. I did not request implementation source.\n\n**Constructor default and setter**\n- `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` checks that `OutlineItem::new` starts with `name_span == None`.\n- The same test checks that `with_name_span(Span::new(7, 8))` sets `Some(Span::new(7, 8))`, and that the value survives `Outline::new` and `clone`.\n\n**Highlight lies within the item's own span, in the original snapshot**\n- `src/outline/contents.rs#mod[tests]/fn[provider_name_spans_match_selector_kind_and_item_boundaries]` runs `validate_outline` on a table of cases. The item span is `(1,3)` over the source `\"_é_\"`.\n- A named selector with `Some(1,3)` is accepted.\n- A named selector with `None` is rejected.\n- Spans that cut the multibyte character (`(1,2)`), are reversed (`(3,1)`), start before the item (`(0,3)`) or end after it (`(1,4)`) are rejected.\n- An anonymous selector (`part@…`) with `None` is accepted. The same selector with `Some` is rejected.\n\n**Highlighted text may differ from the selector name, and original bytes are retained**\n- `rust_name_spans_highlight_spelling_in_the_original_source` shows the impl selector `impl[Wrapper<T>/Trait]` against the highlighted text `Trait for Wrapper<T>`.\n- The same test shows `extern[Rust]` against the highlighted text `extern`, plus `café` with a non-ASCII name.\n- It also checks that the anonymous `use` item has `None`.\n- `language_name_spans_retain_original_bytes_when_selectors_are_normalized` slices the original source with each span. It covers Python, TypeScript (including duplicate names that get distinct spans), TSX, CSS (original whitespace kept), TOML (quoted key kept), YAML, Markdown (anonymous items `None`, heading text kept) and plain text (`None`).\n- That test also asserts that every root has `name_span == None`.\n\n**Gaps**\nThe tests exercise named items, anonymous items and the root. They exercise every language provider and the validation boundaries. I found no obligation without a test that asserts its outcome, and all the tests carry `{test}` references to the subject.",
  "references": "95b9a053855ea953eb6af19f395a452db45b2167f09b99e56da47f18c6e1bdc4",
